as Ashutosh said, we
don't want to be doing

00:13:01.540 --> 00:13:03.970
all of the core infrastructure.

00:13:03.970 --> 00:13:07.810
We want to be solving business
problems core to our business,

00:13:07.810 --> 00:13:10.670
even though we've been
around for a while.

00:13:10.670 --> 00:13:12.640
And so we had a
history of having

00:13:12.640 --> 00:13:16.990
to build things ourselves,
like CI/CD and Search.

00:13:16.990 --> 00:13:19.990
We actually developed
Solr and released that.

00:13:19.990 --> 00:13:21.710
But now we don't
have to do that,

00:13:21.710 --> 00:13:25.370
and we don't really
want to be doing that.

00:13:25.370 --> 00:13:26.900
Security, of course.

00:13:26.900 --> 00:13:29.540
For a long time, there was
this mantra of, oh, the cloud

00:13:29.540 --> 00:13:30.643
is not secure.

00:13:30.643 --> 00:13:32.060
That's not actually
the case, it's

00:13:32.060 --> 00:13:34.390
just you have to think
about it in a different way.

00:13:34.390 --> 00:13:36.972
You can't just draw a nice
ring around your data center.

00:13:36.972 --> 00:13:38.930
You actually have to
think about the components

00:13:38.930 --> 00:13:41.540
and how you're going
to secure them.

00:13:41.540 --> 00:13:44.290
We could leverage
Google's network.

00:13:44.290 --> 00:13:48.690
Their global network can get
really close to our customers

00:13:48.690 --> 00:13:50.490
much faster.

00:13:50.490 --> 00:13:54.440
And we also leveraged
direct connect from our data

00:13:54.440 --> 00:13:57.430
center into GCP.

00:13:57.430 --> 00:14:03.570
And that was really important
for us as we did our migration.

00:14:03.570 --> 00:14:07.760
And then, of course, high
availability disaster recovery.

00:14:07.760 --> 00:14:10.860
We were running in a single
data center in Phoenix.

00:14:10.860 --> 00:14:12.770
So, of course, there's
problems with that.

00:14:12.770 --> 00:14:15.980
If something happened to a core
networking router or something

00:14:15.980 --> 00:14:19.280
like that, it could take down
everything and take us a while

00:14:19.280 --> 00:14:21.260
to recover from that.

00:14:21.260 --> 00:14:25.160
Google, we can spin things up
in multiple zones and regions.

00:14:25.160 --> 00:14:27.450
I will admit, when
we first launched,

00:14:27.450 --> 00:14:29.810
we actually went out in
one region, one zone.

00:14:29.810 --> 00:14:32.030
We did like for like,
and that was really just

00:14:32.030 --> 00:14:34.190
to keep the scope of
what we were trying

00:14:34.190 --> 00:14:36.620
to do as small as possible.

00:14:36.620 --> 00:14:40.150
But soon after we launched,
we went multi-zone,

00:14:40.150 --> 00:14:43.970
and that was relatively
quick and painless

00:14:43.970 --> 00:14:47.420
for the people who
actually did the work.

00:14:47.420 --> 00:14:49.150
So how do we do it?

00:14:49.150 --> 00:14:51.958
I'll bring Ashutosh's
slide back up here.

00:14:51.958 --> 00:14:53.750
And we basically went
through this process.

00:14:53.750 --> 00:14:56.050
We assessed what
our applications

00:14:56.050 --> 00:14:58.690
were doing, what our systems
and infrastructure were.

00:14:58.690 --> 00:15:00.245
We planned what we could do--

00:15:00.245 --> 00:15:02.120
what we could move and
when we could move it,

00:15:02.120 --> 00:15:04.780
and looked at all the
dependencies and complexity.

00:15:04.780 --> 00:15:06.280
But we didn't want
to wait too long.

00:15:06.280 --> 00:15:11.050
We wanted to get migrating
because GCP was new to us,

00:15:11.050 --> 00:15:14.370
and we knew that there was a lot
that we probably wouldn't know

00:15:14.370 --> 00:15:16.413
and we were going to have
to learn along the way.

00:15:16.413 --> 00:15:17.830
And then, of course,
we optimized.

00:15:17.830 --> 00:15:21.380
Like I said, we went
multi-zone afterwards.

00:15:21.380 --> 00:15:25.600
So we went to set off to figure
out what we were going to do,

00:15:25.600 --> 00:15:28.910
how we wanted to do
it for each system.

00:15:28.910 --> 00:15:31.720
We came up with a design
plan for each one.

00:15:31.720 --> 00:15:34.960
Could we leverage
managed services?

00:15:34.960 --> 00:15:37.480
If not, did we have
the expertise in-house

00:15:37.480 --> 00:15:40.300
to set up those systems
and maintain them?

00:15:40.300 --> 00:15:42.190
For example, we
didn't really want

00:15:42.190 --> 00:15:44.210
to set up and maintain
our own Hadoop clusters,

00:15:44.210 --> 00:15:50.500
so we decided to leverage
Dataproc as a managed service.

00:15:50.500 --> 00:15:53.260
But some of those
decisions required, then,

00:15:53.260 --> 00:15:57.190
some refactoring and had impact.

00:15:57.190 --> 00:15:59.860
We knew we could
containerize, run in VMs,

00:15:59.860 --> 00:16:01.960
so some of the things
we wanted to do

00:16:01.960 --> 00:16:04.910
was pretty straightforward.

00:16:04.910 --> 00:16:07.490
And then, once we had a
rough idea of the design,

00:16:07.490 --> 00:16:11.560
we would compare that with
a straight lift-and-shift

00:16:11.560 --> 00:16:14.350
to see if just doing a
straight lift-and-shift

00:16:14.350 --> 00:16:17.260
was actually more
cost effective.

00:16:17.260 --> 00:16:20.928
But I will say, we were kind of
morally opposed to doing just

00:16:20.928 --> 00:16:21.970
straight lift-and-shifts.

00:16:21.970 --> 00:16:27.640
We wanted to gain some wins
out of moving into the cloud

00:16:27.640 --> 00:16:30.230
and not end up with
the same thing,

00:16:30.230 --> 00:16:32.710
just in a different location.

00:16:32.710 --> 00:16:35.290
While we were doing that,
a broader steering group

00:16:35.290 --> 00:16:38.480
was solving these problems
across CBS Interactive.

00:16:38.480 --> 00:16:40.480
And these were things
that I didn't want my team

00:16:40.480 --> 00:16:41.730
to have to be responsible for.

00:16:41.730 --> 00:16:43.660
And quite frankly, they
needed to be solved

00:16:43.660 --> 00:16:45.530
across the entire company.

00:16:45.530 --> 00:16:49.768
How are we going to manage
users and their access?

00:16:49.768 --> 00:16:51.560
What was the networking
going to look like?

00:16:51.560 --> 00:16:53.518
How are we going to
connect to the data center?

00:16:53.518 --> 00:16:55.390
How was the network
going to be managed?

00:16:55.390 --> 00:16:56.965
Security, what
were the policies?

00:16:56.965 --> 00:16:58.840
What were those going
to be and how were they

00:16:58.840 --> 00:17:00.062
going to be enforced?

00:17:00.062 --> 00:17:01.270
And then, of course, billing.

00:17:01.270 --> 00:17:03.070
How were we going to
divide up the costs

00:17:03.070 --> 00:17:05.589
to make sure that we did get
the transparency that we really

00:17:05.589 --> 00:17:08.300
wanted?

00:17:08.300 --> 00:17:10.740
At a high level, this is
what our project looked like.

00:17:10.740 --> 00:17:14.240
So as I said, we started with
a design and architecture

00:17:14.240 --> 00:17:17.119
and did that for a
couple of months.

00:17:17.119 --> 00:17:23.450
And then, we decided, as I said,
some of our back end systems

00:17:23.450 --> 00:17:27.349
were much more complex and were
going to require more thought

00:17:27.349 --> 00:17:29.430
and refactoring,
so they were going

00:17:29.430 --> 00:17:31.010
to take a little bit longer.

00:17:31.010 --> 00:17:34.910
We knew that our front end
stack was a little bit simpler

00:17:34.910 --> 00:17:38.280
and we could get that
out more quickly.

00:17:38.280 --> 00:17:41.540
So we decided to break the
project up into two phases.

00:17:41.540 --> 00:17:43.430
And also, I'm kind
of impatient and I

00:17:43.430 --> 00:17:46.520
didn't want us to have to
wait until everything was done

00:17:46.520 --> 00:17:48.200
before we started
learning, so that's

00:17:48.200 --> 00:17:50.550
why we went with two phases.

00:17:50.550 --> 00:17:53.120
The first phase was really
all our front end reads,

00:17:53.120 --> 00:17:57.350
and that allowed traffic to
come in to those systems.

00:17:57.350 --> 00:18:00.650
The writes stayed in our
legacy infrastructure.

00:18:00.650 --> 00:18:05.270
This also gave us the ability
to slowly ramp up traffic

00:18:05.270 --> 00:18:07.460
into the cloud,
so we didn't have

00:18:07.460 --> 00:18:11.970
to flip a switch, which
was very important to us.

00:18:11.970 --> 00:18:15.180
So this is what the phase
one architecture looked like.

00:18:15.180 --> 00:18:18.720
You can see there, on
the right, our writes

00:18:18.720 --> 00:18:21.450
were going into our
legacy data center.

00:18:21.450 --> 00:18:26.040
And then our legacy web apps
were still serving traffic

00:18:26.040 --> 00:18:27.660
there from those data sources.

00:18:27.660 --> 00:18:31.470
We were replicating those
data sources over into GCP

00:18:31.470 --> 00:18:33.090
through that direct connect.

00:18:33.090 --> 00:18:36.630
And then we had our
containerized applications

00:18:36.630 --> 00:18:38.850
sitting in GCP.

00:18:38.850 --> 00:18:41.580
And then we used
our CDN as a proxy

00:18:41.580 --> 00:18:46.290
to slowly ramp traffic
up over to GCP.

00:18:46.290 --> 00:18:49.470
Over the course of a
few weeks, we did that.

00:18:49.470 --> 00:18:55.380
And then we had 100% of
our traffic running in GCP.

00:18:55.380 --> 00:18:56.580
I'll put an asterisk there.

00:18:56.580 --> 00:19:00.120
It was almost all of our
traffic, all the reads.

00:19:00.120 --> 00:19:02.370
So then we were able to--

00:19:02.370 --> 00:19:04.960
when all of our back end
systems were ready to go,

00:19:04.960 --> 00:19:06.780
then we already had
most of the traffic

00:19:06.780 --> 00:19:10.540
over there, so actually the
launch went really smoothly.

00:19:10.540 --> 00:19:12.660
We were able to break
replication, turn

00:19:12.660 --> 00:19:16.680
on those new write services to
point at those replicas that

00:19:16.680 --> 00:19:17.970
were now the masters.

00:19:17.970 --> 00:19:21.390
And this is what
we ended up with.

00:19:21.390 --> 00:19:23.820
Doesn't this look way
better than that first

00:19:23.820 --> 00:19:27.020
graphic I showed you guys?

00:19:27.020 --> 00:19:30.300
And then coming back to this,
this is actually phase 0.5,

00:19:30.300 --> 00:19:31.830
so this is our
infrastructure setup

00:19:31.830 --> 00:19:35.160
before we started
proxying traffic over.

00:19:35.160 --> 00:19:37.200
We wanted a way to
load test and do

00:19:37.200 --> 00:19:39.880
functional testing
of that system,

00:19:39.880 --> 00:19:42.660
so the team developed
a replay system

00:19:42.660 --> 00:19:46.170
that could take our traffic
using Stackdriver, Cloud

00:19:46.170 --> 00:19:48.450
Pub/Sub, and Cloud
Functions to replay

00:19:48.450 --> 00:19:50.340
our traffic in real-time.

00:19:50.340 --> 00:19:52.867
Allowed us to dial
that up and down

00:19:52.867 --> 00:19:54.450
and we were able to
actually, I think,

00:19:54.450 --> 00:19:58.300
run 300% of our traffic
over into the new system.

00:19:58.300 --> 00:20:01.500
So it really gave us a way
to fine-tune and configure

00:20:01.500 --> 00:20:05.070
our new system before
any real users actually

00:20:05.070 --> 00:20:06.510
had to experience it.

00:20:06.510 --> 00:20:08.552
And Jonathan Lee is going
to come up and give you

00:20:08.552 --> 00:20:11.170
a demo of that.

00:20:11.170 --> 00:20:15.460
So, of course, this was a huge
effort, and most importantly,

00:20:15.460 --> 00:20:18.370
a huge team effort
and a ton of details

00:20:18.370 --> 00:20:21.310
that I haven't even gotten
into, and Jonathan will start

00:20:21.310 --> 00:20:23.080
to get into some of those.

00:20:23.080 --> 00:20:25.990
It required a ton of focus,
collaboration across my team,

00:20:25.990 --> 00:20:28.753
across CBS Interactive,
with Google.

00:20:28.753 --> 00:20:30.670
We also had some partners
that we worked with.

00:20:30.670 --> 00:20:34.760
Deloitte provided some
contractors for us.

00:20:34.760 --> 00:20:36.630
And I'd like to say
thank you to them.

00:20:36.630 --> 00:20:39.880
Many of them are in the
room today, so thank you.

00:20:39.880 --> 00:20:42.310
One of which, who is a star
student sitting in the front

00:20:42.310 --> 00:20:43.690
here, is Jonathan Lee.

00:20:43.690 --> 00:20:46.360
He's director of Dev Ops
for the CNET Media Group.

00:20:46.360 --> 00:20:48.370
I'd like to bring
him up and take you

00:20:48.370 --> 00:20:49.537
through some of the details.

00:20:49.537 --> 00:20:51.787
JONATHAN LEE: This is one
of the critical systems that

00:20:51.787 --> 00:20:52.690
makes CNET CNET.

00:20:52.690 --> 00:20:56.170
This is our catalog
back end system.

00:20:56.170 --> 00:20:59.710
So just wanted to call it
out along with pricing, which

00:20:59.710 --> 00:21:03.340
is a similar system,
but both systems

00:21:03.340 --> 00:21:05.920
take advantage of a lot
of managed services.

00:21:05.920 --> 00:21:09.640
So they're taking advantage
of BigQuery, Dataproc, App

00:21:09.640 --> 00:21:15.430
Engine for Cron, preemptible
VMs, Pub/Sub, and then

00:21:15.430 --> 00:21:16.150
Cloud Storage.

00:21:20.640 --> 00:21:25.490
And then building on top of
that, for our testing strategy,

00:21:25.490 --> 00:21:27.700
this is a front end proxy.

00:21:27.700 --> 00:21:31.567
So this is where all of the user
traffic came in from our users.

00:21:31.567 --> 00:21:34.150
It came in through Akamai, where
we did that traffic splitting

00:21:34.150 --> 00:21:35.680
that John covered.

00:21:35.680 --> 00:21:39.250
It hit the Google load
balancers that then proxied over

00:21:39.250 --> 00:21:41.560
to Varnish instances.

00:21:41.560 --> 00:21:44.090
The Varnish instances
did not support HTTPS,

00:21:44.090 --> 00:21:45.430
so we had to use--

00:21:45.430 --> 00:21:48.550
leverage another open
source product called Hitch.

00:21:48.550 --> 00:21:51.970
And what Hitch does is it
just terminates the HTTPS

00:21:51.970 --> 00:21:54.730
and tucks the
Varnish over the box.

00:21:54.730 --> 00:21:59.050
And then from there, we can
talk to the internal Swarm load

00:21:59.050 --> 00:22:01.150
balancers and then
off to container

00:22:01.150 --> 00:22:04.888
LAN for our front end.

00:22:04.888 --> 00:22:07.180
So I want to talk a little
bit about the infrastructure

00:22:07.180 --> 00:22:07.510
tooling.

00:22:07.510 --> 00:22:09.650
So one of the things we wanted
to do during our migration

00:22:09.650 --> 00:22:11.390
to the cloud is to
adopt infrastructure

00:22:11.390 --> 00:22:15.660
as a code that allowed
us to do more automation.

00:22:15.660 --> 00:22:19.250
So we leveraged Jenkins
and HashiCorp products.

00:22:19.250 --> 00:22:23.510
So Jenkins would talk
to Packer and Terraform

00:22:23.510 --> 00:22:25.740
and provision our
compute instances.

00:22:25.740 --> 00:22:27.530
And then Terraform
would actually

00:22:27.530 --> 00:22:32.040
provision out those Packer
images to our infrastructure.

00:22:32.040 --> 00:22:36.410
So we managed to standardize
on Ubuntu as our base image.

00:22:36.410 --> 00:22:41.000
We had three flavors, 1404
for our legacy applications,

00:22:41.000 --> 00:22:44.480
1604 was what we tried to
adopt for most of our newer

00:22:44.480 --> 00:22:47.570
applications and all of
our Docker Swarm stuff.

00:22:47.570 --> 00:22:54.500
And then we started leveraging
1804 as it got released.

00:22:54.500 --> 00:22:57.890
So jumping back to the security
that John mentioned earlier,

00:22:57.890 --> 00:23:00.800
we leveraged a lot of security
in our infrastructure.

00:23:00.800 --> 00:23:03.230
We had Tenable, which
basically just told us

00:23:03.230 --> 00:23:05.780
when we needed to patch the OS.

00:23:05.780 --> 00:23:06.920
We had Signal Science.

00:23:06.920 --> 00:23:09.950
That's basically
set in our Nginx

00:23:09.950 --> 00:23:12.860
and was the WAF for
our applications.

00:23:12.860 --> 00:23:19.550
We had Aqua Sec that did the
container image scanning.

00:23:19.550 --> 00:23:22.040
Vault, which kept
all of our secrets.

00:23:22.040 --> 00:23:25.910
And then we also, which is not
on here, Cigital and Checkmarx,

00:23:25.910 --> 00:23:30.210
which also did static
analysis of the code.

00:23:30.210 --> 00:23:32.630
So what does the security
integration look like?

00:23:32.630 --> 00:23:38.560
So tying in all
those security tools,

00:23:38.560 --> 00:23:40.540
we had Tenable
that would actually

00:23:40.540 --> 00:23:44.410
be baked into the Packer without
registering any of the images.

00:23:44.410 --> 00:23:46.240
That got pushed up
to cloud storage.

00:23:46.240 --> 00:23:50.020
Terraform would actually
push out those Packer images,

00:23:50.020 --> 00:23:56.350
then registering those VMs as
they were spun up with Tenable.

00:23:56.350 --> 00:23:59.200
Then we had Signal Science
running on the compute engines

00:23:59.200 --> 00:24:00.580
inside the Nginx.

00:24:00.580 --> 00:24:02.410
And then, obviously,
Vault could be

00:24:02.410 --> 00:24:07.040
used for shipping any sort
of secrets over to the VMs.

00:24:07.040 --> 00:24:11.930
For containers, building
on top of the last slide,

00:24:11.930 --> 00:24:15.380
we also leveraged Jenkins to
push to our own private Docker

00:24:15.380 --> 00:24:19.130
registry, which we just ran the
open source Docker registry.

00:24:19.130 --> 00:24:22.265
We're in the process
of moving over to GCR,

00:24:22.265 --> 00:24:23.890
so that's another
managed service we're

00:24:23.890 --> 00:24:25.440
going to take advantage of.

00:24:25.440 --> 00:24:30.440
We use Nexus to manage
any sort of artifacts.

00:24:30.440 --> 00:24:33.020
And then as Jenkins was pushing
out those containers to Swarm

00:24:33.020 --> 00:24:34.520
during that build
process, we would

00:24:34.520 --> 00:24:36.770
leverage Aqua Sec to do
the image scanning to make

00:24:36.770 --> 00:24:38.510
sure they were in compliance.

00:24:38.510 --> 00:24:41.840
And then we also ran
Aqua Sec enforcer inside

00:24:41.840 --> 00:24:43.652
of the Swarm clusters,
which could tell us

00:24:43.652 --> 00:24:45.485
if any of the containers
were out of policy.

00:24:49.110 --> 00:24:54.170
So as for Docker services, most
of our stateless applications

00:24:54.170 --> 00:24:54.980
were Dockerized.

00:24:54.980 --> 00:24:57.500
And we had a pretty
standard setup

00:24:57.500 --> 00:25:00.020
where we tried to follow
best practices, which

00:25:00.020 --> 00:25:02.900
is one process per container.

00:25:02.900 --> 00:25:05.130
So we had containers
that ran the Nginx,

00:25:05.130 --> 00:25:07.790
and then those actually talked
internally in the Swarm cluster

00:25:07.790 --> 00:25:13.153
over to PHP, Python, whatever
other application languages

00:25:13.153 --> 00:25:13.820
we were running.

00:25:16.810 --> 00:25:20.460
So now I want to get into
three of the things that

00:25:20.460 --> 00:25:24.090
are most critical for
any cloud migration,

00:25:24.090 --> 00:25:26.860
and this should be useful
for just about everyone.

00:25:26.860 --> 00:25:28.172
So the first one is logging.

00:25:28.172 --> 00:25:30.630
You have to have logging to be
able to migrate to the cloud

00:25:30.630 --> 00:25:31.960
successfully.

00:25:31.960 --> 00:25:34.360
So before we started
our GCP migration,

00:25:34.360 --> 00:25:39.120
we decided to integrate a
lot of our tooling into GCP

00:25:39.120 --> 00:25:41.220
before actually moving
the applications over.

00:25:41.220 --> 00:25:45.120
So we needed to get those logs
from the on-prem data center

00:25:45.120 --> 00:25:46.840
into GCP.

00:25:46.840 --> 00:25:50.940
So we tried to leverage
all of the tooling that

00:25:50.940 --> 00:25:54.120
was on the machines without
actually editing any of them

00:25:54.120 --> 00:25:56.350
or adding any new software
or anything like that.

00:25:56.350 --> 00:25:59.430
So for Solr, for
instance, we had

00:25:59.430 --> 00:26:02.220
our syslog running on the VMs
inside of our data center.

00:26:02.220 --> 00:26:05.010
We just had those remotely
ship those logs over

00:26:05.010 --> 00:26:07.980
to Fluentd boxes that
were running in GCP.

00:26:07.980 --> 00:26:11.730
Those Fluentd boxes basically
just proxied those over

00:26:11.730 --> 00:26:15.030
and authorized them
with the GCP platform

00:26:15.030 --> 00:26:18.510
and then shipped those over
to Stackdriver Logging.

00:26:18.510 --> 00:26:20.670
And then, for
containerized workload,

00:26:20.670 --> 00:26:24.570
we leveraged another open source
logging driver called logspout.

00:26:24.570 --> 00:26:27.480
And that effectively
did the same thing

00:26:27.480 --> 00:26:31.830
that our syslogs did, which
is ship the logs over to GCP.

00:26:31.830 --> 00:26:34.890
So once they got into GCP,
got into Stackdriver Logging,

00:26:34.890 --> 00:26:38.340
we now had visibility across
our on-prem data center

00:26:38.340 --> 00:26:42.570
using GCP's Stackdriver Logging.

00:26:42.570 --> 00:26:45.210
So from there, we
decided that, well, we

00:26:45.210 --> 00:26:48.345
need to get people
on board with GCP.

00:26:48.345 --> 00:26:50.220
A lot of people haven't
been trained on that,

00:26:50.220 --> 00:26:54.870
so we decided to leverage
another architecture.

00:26:54.870 --> 00:26:58.552
How many people know
about the ELK Stack?

00:26:58.552 --> 00:27:00.510
So for the people that
don't know what that is,

00:27:00.510 --> 00:27:04.350
that's a Elasticsearch,
Logstash, and Kibana.

00:27:04.350 --> 00:27:07.290
Logstash actually parses
the logs, ships them

00:27:07.290 --> 00:27:09.180
to Elasticsearch,
which stores them,

00:27:09.180 --> 00:27:10.830
and then Kibana's
like a pretty GUI

00:27:10.830 --> 00:27:13.170
that sits on the front of it.

00:27:13.170 --> 00:27:17.610
So we ship the Stackdriver
logs over to Pub/Sub.

00:27:17.610 --> 00:27:20.910
From there, that got
pulled from Logstash

00:27:20.910 --> 00:27:25.110
and then got ingested
into that ELK Stack.

00:27:25.110 --> 00:27:30.490
Once we migrated over to GCP,
fast forward a few months,

00:27:30.490 --> 00:27:35.880
we were able to remove
that Fluentd dummy boxes

00:27:35.880 --> 00:27:39.540
and just ship straight into
Stackdriver Logging, so not

00:27:39.540 --> 00:27:40.600
much change there.

00:27:40.600 --> 00:27:43.568
So here, we kind of have
just the two logging systems.

00:27:43.568 --> 00:27:45.360
We have the Stackdriver
Logging that people

00:27:45.360 --> 00:27:48.293
were getting ramped up on
on how to use and leverage.

00:27:48.293 --> 00:27:49.710
And then over on
the right, we had

00:27:49.710 --> 00:27:54.810
Kibana, which is a bit
more friendly to new people

00:27:54.810 --> 00:27:57.030
coming into logging.

00:27:57.030 --> 00:28:01.320
So we'll go over that a
little bit in the demo.

00:28:01.320 --> 00:28:03.780
So after you have
logging in place,

00:28:03.780 --> 00:28:06.510
the next most critical thing
is monitoring, obviously,

00:28:06.510 --> 00:28:09.420
so you need to know what
your systems are doing, where

00:28:09.420 --> 00:28:13.020
the errors are coming in, when
you need to look at the logs

00:28:13.020 --> 00:28:14.740
and dig in.

00:28:14.740 --> 00:28:17.850
So we leveraged quite a
few open source products,

00:28:17.850 --> 00:28:23.400
Prometheus being the big one,
and then Grafana for displaying

00:28:23.400 --> 00:28:26.110
those Prometheus metrics.

00:28:26.110 --> 00:28:29.845
And then, also with the
alerting, you need--

00:28:29.845 --> 00:28:31.480
in the monitoring,
you need alerting.

00:28:31.480 --> 00:28:36.040
So we had Node Advisor for
the system-level metrics.

00:28:36.040 --> 00:28:37.900
That got shipped into
Prometheus. cAdvisor

00:28:37.900 --> 00:28:39.310
for container-level metrics.

00:28:39.310 --> 00:28:41.050
And then we had a
Blackbox exporter

00:28:41.050 --> 00:28:43.600
that could actually kind of
work similar to a Pingdom

00:28:43.600 --> 00:28:46.662
where it can just do a health
check to the application

00:28:46.662 --> 00:28:48.120
to make sure that
they're still up.

00:28:51.080 --> 00:28:53.570
On the front end
side, we didn't really

00:28:53.570 --> 00:28:55.970
have to worry too much
about front end performance,

00:28:55.970 --> 00:28:58.790
but we did still test it just
to make sure that we weren't

00:28:58.790 --> 00:29:00.510
going to impact end users.

00:29:00.510 --> 00:29:02.780
But we leveraged a
couple other open source

00:29:02.780 --> 00:29:05.750
tools, one being sitespeed.io.

00:29:05.750 --> 00:29:08.300
And then that integrated
with WebPageTest.

00:29:08.300 --> 00:29:11.330
So we ran those side by
side comparing the front end

00:29:11.330 --> 00:29:14.150
performance of our legacy
application and our GCP

00:29:14.150 --> 00:29:17.110
application.

00:29:17.110 --> 00:29:20.080
And then, last but not
least for the front end,

00:29:20.080 --> 00:29:22.690
we also integrated with
Akamai mPulse, which

00:29:22.690 --> 00:29:25.780
allowed us to get real user
monitoring from the end users'

00:29:25.780 --> 00:29:28.800
browsers while we were
doing that migration,

00:29:28.800 --> 00:29:30.550
so we could actually
lay them side by side

00:29:30.550 --> 00:29:32.500
and we could see if
there was any performance

00:29:32.500 --> 00:29:33.790
problems to our end users.

00:29:37.090 --> 00:29:38.910
So with all that in
place, we decided

00:29:38.910 --> 00:29:41.670
that we needed to get
more experience in GCP,

00:29:41.670 --> 00:29:44.928
so we decided to do
table top exercises.

00:29:44.928 --> 00:29:46.470
And within those
table top exercises,

00:29:46.470 --> 00:29:49.590
we could do anything from
just stopping servers

00:29:49.590 --> 00:29:52.380
to give people that operational
experience of debugging

00:29:52.380 --> 00:29:55.290
and figuring out
what's going on.

00:29:55.290 --> 00:29:58.620
Bad code deployments,
thundering herd problem.

00:29:58.620 --> 00:30:01.190
How many people
have heard of that?

00:30:01.190 --> 00:30:01.980
Any?

00:30:01.980 --> 00:30:03.878
One, two.

00:30:03.878 --> 00:30:05.670
So the thundering herd
problem is basically

00:30:05.670 --> 00:30:07.538
just that you stop
all the traffic.

00:30:07.538 --> 00:30:10.080
And then, all of a sudden, you
just send a bunch of traffic--

00:30:10.080 --> 00:30:13.000
like exponential
amount of traffic.

00:30:13.000 --> 00:30:16.470
So we tend to have live events,
things like CES and Black

00:30:16.470 --> 00:30:17.680
Friday and things like that.

00:30:17.680 --> 00:30:21.210
So we'll get a surge of traffic
if an iPhone gets released

00:30:21.210 --> 00:30:22.510
or something like that.

00:30:22.510 --> 00:30:24.270
So we wanted to
test that scenario.

00:30:26.790 --> 00:30:30.200
So let's get into one of
the testing strategies

00:30:30.200 --> 00:30:31.640
that we came up
internally, which

00:30:31.640 --> 00:30:36.620
was the live replay
of production traffic.

00:30:36.620 --> 00:30:39.650
So we brainstormed this
in-house after evaluating

00:30:39.650 --> 00:30:42.740
with several external third
parties and open source

00:30:42.740 --> 00:30:43.910
products.

00:30:43.910 --> 00:30:47.240
They didn't quite fit
our needs internally.

00:30:47.240 --> 00:30:51.710
We wanted to replicate live
traffic, not just simply replay

00:30:51.710 --> 00:30:55.610
a percentage of a specific page
or types of page or anything

00:30:55.610 --> 00:30:56.910
that's static like that.

00:30:56.910 --> 00:30:59.120
So a lot of the
open source tools

00:30:59.120 --> 00:31:01.670
allow you to replay things
like an article page

00:31:01.670 --> 00:31:05.390
at 10%, reviews pages at 70%.

00:31:05.390 --> 00:31:06.830
So it was very
static and you had

00:31:06.830 --> 00:31:10.190
to pass at a static
list of URLs.

00:31:10.190 --> 00:31:14.270
So going back to that
front end diagram,

00:31:14.270 --> 00:31:16.460
we leveraged that
front end entry

00:31:16.460 --> 00:31:18.610
point for our replay test.

00:31:18.610 --> 00:31:20.240
And we realized that
all we had to do

00:31:20.240 --> 00:31:22.340
to test the entire
system in the end

00:31:22.340 --> 00:31:28.580
is really just send user traffic
like real users were coming in.

00:31:28.580 --> 00:31:32.350
So let's walk through the
system that we built internally.

00:31:32.350 --> 00:31:34.990
So obviously, you have
the end users there

00:31:34.990 --> 00:31:37.900
on the right talking to our
legacy Phoenix data center

00:31:37.900 --> 00:31:38.980
servers.

00:31:38.980 --> 00:31:41.890
From there, we're
shipping those logs

00:31:41.890 --> 00:31:47.290
using our syslog or logspout
over to Fluentd boxes in GCP.

00:31:47.290 --> 00:31:49.870
From there, they get congested
in Stackdriver Logging.

00:31:49.870 --> 00:31:51.670
From there, Pub/Sub.

00:31:51.670 --> 00:31:53.950
Pub/Sub can actually
kick off cloud functions.

00:31:53.950 --> 00:31:55.570
And then within the
cloud functions,

00:31:55.570 --> 00:31:58.180
we actually had the ability
to do any sort of programming

00:31:58.180 --> 00:32:02.260
or manipulation of those
logs as they came in.

00:32:02.260 --> 00:32:05.980
So we could introduce
errors, we could dynamically

00:32:05.980 --> 00:32:07.990
just replay live traffic.

00:32:07.990 --> 00:32:13.000
We could even time box
and replay certain logs

00:32:13.000 --> 00:32:15.330
if we wanted to.

00:32:15.330 --> 00:32:16.990
And then from there,
the cloud function

00:32:16.990 --> 00:32:21.520
would actually issue those HTTP
calls over to the GCP boxes.

00:32:21.520 --> 00:32:25.780
So this entire system allowed
us to dynamically replay

00:32:25.780 --> 00:32:28.120
any sort of traffic coming
into Stackdriver Logging

00:32:28.120 --> 00:32:29.830
and replay it to a
secondary system.

00:32:34.340 --> 00:32:38.084
This is our just
normal CNET.com.

00:32:38.084 --> 00:32:40.250
I pushed it up to CNETNext.

00:32:43.477 --> 00:32:45.310
So what we want to do
is we want to leverage

00:32:45.310 --> 00:32:50.840
that sweet retro CNET that
John mentioned earlier.

00:32:50.840 --> 00:32:54.750
So what I did is I pulled
a retro.CNETNext.com.

00:32:54.750 --> 00:32:56.560
And what we're
going to do is we're

00:32:56.560 --> 00:32:59.560
going to send traffic to
this one using those cloud

00:32:59.560 --> 00:33:00.400
functions.

00:33:00.400 --> 00:33:02.320
We're going to replay
that traffic over

00:33:02.320 --> 00:33:04.600
to the retro system.

00:33:04.600 --> 00:33:07.820
So jumping over to
the cloud console,

00:33:07.820 --> 00:33:10.150
you can see I've already
pre-baked all the VMs.

00:33:10.150 --> 00:33:14.230
We have the legacy
Swarm cluster that's

00:33:14.230 --> 00:33:16.540
running that CNETNext.com.

00:33:16.540 --> 00:33:18.430
Then we also have a
GKE cluster that's

00:33:18.430 --> 00:33:21.250
running the retro.CNETNext.com.

00:33:21.250 --> 00:33:24.820
And then, also, I have
a separate cluster

00:33:24.820 --> 00:33:28.420
that's running tools, so
Grafana, the ELK Stack, things

00:33:28.420 --> 00:33:30.520
like that.

00:33:30.520 --> 00:33:33.800
So reconnect to that.

00:33:33.800 --> 00:33:36.260
So here we got the
Kubernetes cluster.

00:33:36.260 --> 00:33:39.440
It's got four nodes in
it, pretty standard stuff.

00:33:42.490 --> 00:33:44.620
Jumping into one
of the workers, we

00:33:44.620 --> 00:33:47.530
can actually look at the logs.

00:33:47.530 --> 00:33:50.380
So going to hit Play on that.

00:33:50.380 --> 00:33:54.630
So you can see I'm actually
leveraging another tool.

00:33:54.630 --> 00:33:57.280
It's similar to Siege or Vegeta.

00:33:57.280 --> 00:33:58.960
If anybody is
familiar with those,

00:33:58.960 --> 00:34:00.670
what you can do
with those is you

00:34:00.670 --> 00:34:06.500
can just send traffic
directly to a system,

00:34:06.500 --> 00:34:08.230
and you can set a rate on that.

00:34:08.230 --> 00:34:10.060
So we're sending
over traffic just

00:34:10.060 --> 00:34:13.239
in case we don't
get enough hits.

00:34:13.239 --> 00:34:17.659
So from there, we can
see those logs are coming

00:34:17.659 --> 00:34:20.710
in to that legacy system.

00:34:20.710 --> 00:34:23.699
And we can actually see a few
people actually coming in.

00:34:23.699 --> 00:34:25.710
But you can see that
it's the actual Vegeta

00:34:25.710 --> 00:34:29.699
system, because it has
the go HTTP client.

00:34:32.560 --> 00:34:35.590
And then from there, we
can export out to Pub/Sub.

00:34:35.590 --> 00:34:38.530
So we have a traffic
replay export

00:34:38.530 --> 00:34:41.980
that's shipping over
to the Pub/Sub topics.

00:34:41.980 --> 00:34:44.208
Just a note, if you
do have exports out

00:34:44.208 --> 00:34:46.000
and you want to save
a little bit of money,

00:34:46.000 --> 00:34:50.020
you can actually put in
exclusions on your logs

00:34:50.020 --> 00:34:52.420
so that they don't actually
get stored into Stackdriver

00:34:52.420 --> 00:34:55.147
Logging, but they will still
get shipped over to Pub/Sub.

00:34:55.147 --> 00:34:56.980
So we do that to save
a little bit of money.

00:34:56.980 --> 00:34:59.500
It's disabled right now.

00:34:59.500 --> 00:35:01.350
So on the topic, we have--

00:35:01.350 --> 00:35:06.060
the topic from the export,
from Stackdriver Logging,

00:35:06.060 --> 00:35:07.770
it has two subscriptions
in it right now.

00:35:11.165 --> 00:35:12.040
We jump over to that.

00:35:12.040 --> 00:35:15.370
One is actually shipping
over to that cloud function,

00:35:15.370 --> 00:35:17.940
and the other one is actually
getting pulled from an ELK

00:35:17.940 --> 00:35:21.380
Stack that I'm actually running
in that tools Swarm cluster.

00:35:21.380 --> 00:35:26.740
So if we take a look over
at those tools workers,

00:35:26.740 --> 00:35:29.170
we actually have an ELK Stack
running in there pulling

00:35:29.170 --> 00:35:32.506
those logs in real time.

00:35:32.506 --> 00:35:34.340
I'm going to refresh
on here so you can see.

00:35:36.930 --> 00:35:39.800
So we can see those logs
are coming in in real time.

00:35:39.800 --> 00:35:42.620
And what that allows us
to do for the developers,

00:35:42.620 --> 00:35:45.950
is this gives them an easy user
interface for them to get up

00:35:45.950 --> 00:35:48.860
and running with
filtering logs, so

00:35:48.860 --> 00:35:50.690
they get these nice
little buttons where

00:35:50.690 --> 00:35:55.680
they can include and
exclude different filters

00:35:55.680 --> 00:35:59.590
and track down any
sort of issues.

00:35:59.590 --> 00:36:01.960
So jumping over to
the replay system,

00:36:01.960 --> 00:36:05.040
so now we have a cloud
function that's actually

00:36:05.040 --> 00:36:07.330
getting messages from the logs.

00:36:07.330 --> 00:36:10.320
So you can see here we have that
cloud function actually running

00:36:10.320 --> 00:36:11.600
right now.

00:36:11.600 --> 00:36:17.760
It's issuing about 50 requests
a second over to the new system.

00:36:17.760 --> 00:36:19.220
If we jump into
the source code--

00:36:19.220 --> 00:36:21.220
going to dive a little
bit into the source code.

00:36:21.220 --> 00:36:23.440
The source code is
pretty straightforward.

00:36:23.440 --> 00:36:27.420
We're using Node 8 and
using request promises.

00:36:27.420 --> 00:36:30.420
So with those requests
promises, we're

00:36:30.420 --> 00:36:33.330
setting up the domain,
setting up the user agent.

00:36:33.330 --> 00:36:37.140
We can actually set a
percentage inside the node code

00:36:37.140 --> 00:36:40.350
and replay a certain percentage
of that traffic that's

00:36:40.350 --> 00:36:42.010
coming into that legacy system.

00:36:42.010 --> 00:36:45.510
So here we have it set to 10%.

00:36:45.510 --> 00:36:48.120
And we're only replaying 10%
of the 100% traffic that's

00:36:48.120 --> 00:36:50.200
coming into the legacy system.

00:36:50.200 --> 00:36:53.640
So down here, we
set up the request.

00:36:53.640 --> 00:36:55.282
We can put some
headers on there.

00:36:55.282 --> 00:36:56.740
We actually changed
the user agent,

00:36:56.740 --> 00:37:00.160
so we know it's coming from
the traffic replay system.

00:37:00.160 --> 00:37:04.350
And then we can use the promises
and catch those promises,

00:37:04.350 --> 00:37:07.350
whether it's successful
or if it fails.

00:37:07.350 --> 00:37:11.090
So jumping over to the
logs, we can actually

00:37:11.090 --> 00:37:14.980
watch the logs from the
cloud functions in real time.

00:37:14.980 --> 00:37:17.430
And we can see when the
functions are executing

00:37:17.430 --> 00:37:22.100
and how long they're taking
and that sort of thing.

00:37:22.100 --> 00:37:25.640
Jumping back to the
Kubernetes cluster,

00:37:25.640 --> 00:37:28.350
we can actually look
at the services.

00:37:28.350 --> 00:37:32.150
We can see that CNET service
that's running the retro site.

00:37:32.150 --> 00:37:37.400
We can look at those Stackdriver
logs from the new system.

00:37:37.400 --> 00:37:40.280
And we can see the cloud
function is actually

00:37:40.280 --> 00:37:43.297
coming in in real time.

00:37:43.297 --> 00:37:44.880
So I'm going to jump
over here and I'm

00:37:44.880 --> 00:37:48.630
going to change this
real quick from 10.

00:37:51.678 --> 00:37:53.970
So I'm going to edit the
cloud function just so that we

00:37:53.970 --> 00:37:56.020
can see this here in a second.

00:37:56.020 --> 00:37:58.748
I'm going to change it to 100%.

00:37:58.748 --> 00:38:00.410
Then I'm going to deploy that.

00:38:00.410 --> 00:38:03.050
So while that's going, it's
going to change the replay

00:38:03.050 --> 00:38:08.080
traffic over from 10% to 100%.

00:38:08.080 --> 00:38:10.010
So we have our Prometheus
instances running.

00:38:10.010 --> 00:38:12.560
That's capturing
all of our metrics.

00:38:12.560 --> 00:38:15.290
We have Alertmanager that we
can actually fire off alerts

00:38:15.290 --> 00:38:19.940
out to Slack or
anything like that.

00:38:19.940 --> 00:38:21.650
I'm leveraging unsee,
which is basically

00:38:21.650 --> 00:38:24.900
a wrapper for Alertmanager that
gives you a nice, pretty GUI.

00:38:24.900 --> 00:38:29.160
So I actually have the tools--

00:38:29.160 --> 00:38:31.180
cluster-- actually
showing a few [INAUDIBLE],,

00:38:31.180 --> 00:38:33.840
so we could see what
that looks like.

00:38:33.840 --> 00:38:34.900
And then Grafana.

00:38:34.900 --> 00:38:37.610
So Grafana is the
big thing here.

00:38:37.610 --> 00:38:39.360
We can actually look
at any of the metrics

00:38:39.360 --> 00:38:43.785
across any of the legacy
clusters or Kubernetes cluster.

00:38:43.785 --> 00:38:45.660
So here we're looking
at the Prometheus stats

00:38:45.660 --> 00:38:48.030
from the tools Swarm
cluster, and we can make sure

00:38:48.030 --> 00:38:50.110
that that's healthy.

00:38:50.110 --> 00:38:54.540
We can look at the individual
Docker Node Swarm elements.

00:38:57.280 --> 00:38:58.930
Let's switch over to the mic.

00:39:01.940 --> 00:39:03.680
So oh-- OK.

00:39:06.330 --> 00:39:09.593
We can look at the Swarm
node, the nodes themselves,

00:39:09.593 --> 00:39:11.510
and we can actually see
the uptime of how long

00:39:11.510 --> 00:39:14.800
those hosts are running
and any sort of system load

00:39:14.800 --> 00:39:17.150
or performance that
was running on those.

00:39:17.150 --> 00:39:19.890
Same thing for the
Swarm services.

00:39:19.890 --> 00:39:22.760
We can see how many
replicas are running inside

00:39:22.760 --> 00:39:25.280
of that Swarm cluster.

00:39:25.280 --> 00:39:27.770
And then also, over on
the Kubernetes side,

00:39:27.770 --> 00:39:31.093
we can look at Grafana
dashboards for the CNET site,

00:39:31.093 --> 00:39:33.260
for that retro site, and
see how many containers are

00:39:33.260 --> 00:39:35.390
running inside of Kubernetes.

00:39:35.390 --> 00:39:38.390
Same thing-- with
Kubernetes or Swarm,

00:39:38.390 --> 00:39:40.340
you can actually start
pulling in metrics

00:39:40.340 --> 00:39:43.250
and start calculating cost and
build dashboards around that,

00:39:43.250 --> 00:39:45.810
which is pretty neat.

00:39:45.810 --> 00:39:49.190
And then finally, we can
mash all that up together

00:39:49.190 --> 00:39:53.370
and we can create a replay
dashboard that actually shows

00:39:53.370 --> 00:39:55.370
our Swarm cluster on the
left and our Kubernetes

00:39:55.370 --> 00:39:58.730
cluster on the right, and we can
actually watch it in real time

00:39:58.730 --> 00:40:00.360
as traffic is coming in.

00:40:00.360 --> 00:40:05.220
So as we scroll in down here,
we can see on the Swarm,

00:40:05.220 --> 00:40:08.010
we have the containers running.

00:40:08.010 --> 00:40:11.100
We can look at the container
CPU, individual containers

00:40:11.100 --> 00:40:14.850
in Swarm versus the individual
containers in Kubernetes.

00:40:14.850 --> 00:40:18.030
Below that, we can look
at the actual host VM,

00:40:18.030 --> 00:40:22.680
and look at the CPU usage
across the host VMs,

00:40:22.680 --> 00:40:25.993
and then also the nodes
inside of Kubernetes.

00:40:25.993 --> 00:40:27.410
In the middle, we
can see how many

00:40:27.410 --> 00:40:30.410
requests are coming in per
minute from the legacy system

00:40:30.410 --> 00:40:31.560
to the new system.

00:40:31.560 --> 00:40:34.760
And then as we change
that cloud function,

00:40:34.760 --> 00:40:39.000
we can see it react in real
time with those changes.

00:40:39.000 --> 00:40:44.070
And then below that, we can see
requests per minute, per host.

00:40:44.070 --> 00:40:47.432
So we're actually connecting
here over to the ELK Stack

00:40:47.432 --> 00:40:48.140
that I'm running.

00:40:48.140 --> 00:40:51.050
And we're actually pulling
the logs individually

00:40:51.050 --> 00:40:53.060
from the ELK Stack
and then displaying

00:40:53.060 --> 00:40:54.350
those on this dashboard.

00:40:54.350 --> 00:40:57.380
And then below that,
finally, we can actually

00:40:57.380 --> 00:41:00.110
connect to the
Stackdriver monitoring

00:41:00.110 --> 00:41:03.230
and we can actually pull metrics
from those managed services

00:41:03.230 --> 00:41:03.750
as well.

00:41:03.750 --> 00:41:06.200
So here we're actually
looking at the Pub/Sub queue

00:41:06.200 --> 00:41:07.643
of unacknowledged messages.

00:41:07.643 --> 00:41:09.560
And we can see if we're
overloading the system

00:41:09.560 --> 00:41:10.640
with too many messages.

00:41:10.640 --> 00:41:13.130
And obviously, in this
demo, I am since I only

00:41:13.130 --> 00:41:14.000
have one VM running.

00:41:19.652 --> 00:41:20.600
Cool.

00:41:20.600 --> 00:41:23.255
So that's basically it.

00:41:23.255 --> 00:41:25.130
Can we jump back over
to the slides, I guess?

00:41:35.410 --> 00:41:35.910
Cool.

00:41:39.860 --> 00:41:42.360
So what's next?

00:41:42.360 --> 00:41:44.110
I just wanted to call
out some of the tech

00:41:44.110 --> 00:41:47.590
that we actually used on CNET.

00:41:47.590 --> 00:41:49.840
The ones bolded here
are all the tech

00:41:49.840 --> 00:41:52.378
we were able to use
for the CNET migration.

00:41:52.378 --> 00:41:53.920
Obviously, there's
a lot more that we

00:41:53.920 --> 00:41:55.690
can leverage with
the managed services

00:41:55.690 --> 00:41:59.080
as we move off running
our own infrastructure

00:41:59.080 --> 00:42:01.360
and move over to Kubernetes
and things like that.

00:42:06.240 --> 00:42:09.080
And then here's a few
of the upcoming projects

00:42:09.080 --> 00:42:14.760
that we're going to be
working on in 2019/2020.

00:42:14.760 --> 00:42:17.760
Hopefully, leveraging GKE,
Memorystore, things like that,

00:42:17.760 --> 00:42:20.200
so that we don't have
to run our own VMs.

00:42:20.200 --> 00:42:23.040
And obviously, leveraging
that traffic replay system

00:42:23.040 --> 00:42:24.150
that we just went through.

00:42:24.150 --> 00:42:27.500
[MUSIC PLAYING]

